# Production (and staging): Docker Compose's variables. Copy it, then fill it:
#
#   cp env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Keep .env out of git (it is ignored). Every other variable, with its
# default: docs/configuration.md and compose.prod.yaml.
#
# REQUIRED: APP_DOMAIN, ACME_EMAIL, CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD (the last three, or their files: "Secrets in files"
# at the end).
#
# Generate each secret on its own (never reuse one value for two names):
#   passwords (they go into URLs: hex only)   openssl rand -hex 24
#   keys and signing secrets                  openssl rand -base64 32

# Docker Compose: the project (names containers and volumes), its files, how
# many app instances, and the deployment's name (staging: same stack, its own
# .env, APP_ENV=staging). With COMPOSE_FILE set, `docker compose up -d --build`
# needs no -f flags.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=nestjs-monorepo-template
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml
API_REPLICAS=2
WORKER_REPLICAS=1
APP_ENV=production
# Optional: run an image CI published instead of building one here
# (docs/deployment.md, "Publishing and rolling back images"). Then
# `docker compose pull migrate api worker gotenberg caddy && docker compose up -d`, without --build.
# APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>:<full commit SHA>
# And Gotenberg's (PDF documents), the image CI scanned and published:
# GOTENBERG_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>/gotenberg:<full commit SHA>
# And Caddy's (with the web app, the same for every deployment), likewise:
# CADDY_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>/caddy:<full commit SHA>
# Optional: make the deploy scripts verify the images' cosign signature, SBOM and
# provenance first (scripts/verify-image.sh; needs cosign). Off unless 1.
# VERIFY_IMAGES=1
# VERIFY_IDENTITY_REGEXP=  (a different accepted signer; default: this repo's ci.yml)

# Caddy: HTTPS for APP_DOMAIN, through a Cloudflare DNS challenge. REQUIRED.
# APP_DOMAIN: replace the placeholder with the API's host name.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: a Cloudflare token with Zone.DNS edit on the domain's
# zone. Moving from an earlier deployment? Copy the token and email from its
# Caddy container:
#   docker inspect --format '{{json .Config.Env}}' <caddy container>
APP_DOMAIN=api.example.com
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
ACME_EMAIL=

# Caddy also serves /.well-known/security.txt (RFC 9116; docs/deployment.md,
# "Security contact"). Unset SECURITY_CONTACT: no file, the path is a 404.
# SECURITY_CONTACT: mailto: or https: URIs, comma-separated (required by the RFC).
# SECURITY_EXPIRES: RFC 3339 UTC, e.g. 2027-10-21T00:00:00Z; unset, a year
# from each start of Caddy. SECURITY_POLICY: the URL of your SECURITY.md (the
# default is this template's). SECURITY_PREFERRED_LANGUAGES: default en.
# SECURITY_CONTACT=mailto:security@example.com
# SECURITY_EXPIRES=
# SECURITY_POLICY=
# SECURITY_PREFERRED_LANGUAGES=en

# Caddy's maintenance page (docs/deployment.md, "Maintenance page"): on, and
# the web app's paths answer a static "down for maintenance" page (503,
# Retry-After) instead of the app, so not even its shell is served. The API's
# paths are untouched; APP_MODE=maintenance (below) makes them refuse too.
# Exactly `on` turns it on; anything else, or unset, is off. Recreate Caddy to
# change it: docker compose up -d caddy
# MAINTENANCE_PAGE=off

# PostgreSQL of the stack: two roles (docs/deployment.md, "Database roles").
# POSTGRES_USER (default app) owns the tables and runs the migrations and the
# backups; POSTGRES_APP_USER (default app_runtime) is what api and worker
# connect as: it owns nothing, can't change the schema and can't bypass
# row-level security. `migrate` creates it on the first deploy from these
# values. Both passwords are REQUIRED (hex: they go into URLs), or their
# files (DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE and DATABASE_URL_FILE, at the end).
#   openssl rand -hex 24      (one each)
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=
# The maintenance role (POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_USER, default app_maintenance):
# BYPASSRLS, for the worker's sweeps across tenants (retention) and the
# metrics over every tenant; migrate creates it, only the worker connects as
# it (docs/tenancy.md, "The bypass"). Empty: those jobs refuse to run.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_PASSWORD=
# Managed PostgreSQL instead: DATABASE_URL (the app's role) and
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL (the owner: your admin account, with CREATEROLE) replace
# the four above; leave postgres and backup out of the stack.
# DATABASE_URL=
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL=
# Redis: optional but recommended (unset, Redis runs without a password).
#   openssl rand -hex 24
REDIS_PASSWORD=
# The monitoring role (compose.monitoring.yaml's PostgreSQL exporter; REQUIRED
# once that file is in COMPOSE_FILE): POSTGRES_MONITOR_USER (default
# app_monitor) is a member of pg_monitor and nothing else, reads statistics
# and never a row. `migrate` creates it from these values (or sets a changed
# password). Managed PostgreSQL: DATABASE_MONITOR_URL is the exporter's URL
# for the role `migrate` made there.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_MONITOR_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_MONITOR_URL=
# The reporting role for platform analytics (docs/tenancy.md, "Platform
# analytics"): POSTGRES_REPORTING_USER (default app_reporting) reads the
# `analytics` schema's anonymized aggregates and no other table, never a
# tenant's row. `migrate` creates it from these values (or sets a changed
# password); empty: no such role. Connect your BI tool as it.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_REPORTING_PASSWORD=

# PgBouncer, optional (docs/deployment.md, "PgBouncer"): add
# compose.pgbouncer.yaml to COMPOSE_FILE and api and worker connect through it,
# in transaction pooling mode. Server connections per role, the clients it
# accepts, and how long a client waits for a server connection (seconds).
# PGBOUNCER_POOL_SIZE=20
# PGBOUNCER_MAINTENANCE_POOL_SIZE=3
# PGBOUNCER_MAX_CLIENT_CONN=400
# PGBOUNCER_QUERY_WAIT_TIMEOUT=10
# A managed PostgreSQL behind it (default: the stack's own, `postgres`:5432).
# PGBOUNCER_DB_HOST=postgres
# PGBOUNCER_DB_PORT=5432
# PGBOUNCER_CPUS=0.5
# PGBOUNCER_MEMORY=128M

# Backups (docs/deployment.md, "Backups and restore"): the database, and the
# uploaded files with STORAGE_DRIVER=local, on a cron schedule (UTC), kept
# this many days in the backups volume. Unhealthy when the newest successful
# backup is older than BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS (raise it with a rarer schedule).
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=0 3 * * *
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=25
# Off the host, recommended (empty bucket: local copies only, lost with the
# disk). A bucket of its own and keys for it alone, not the app's S3_*; give
# the bucket a lifecycle rule, since the backup never deletes there.
# R2: endpoint https://<account id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, region auto.
# AWS: no endpoint; empty keys use the host's IAM role.
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_REGION=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Encrypt every backup (dumps, files, base backups, WAL) with age before it is
# kept or uploaded, so the bucket never holds plaintext. The PUBLIC key(s)
# (age1…, space separated; or one SSH public key, quoted); keep the identity
# off the server, it is needed to restore only (docs/deployment.md,
# "Encryption of backups"):
#   age-keygen -o backup.key
BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Point-in-time recovery, off unless compose.pitr.yaml is added to COMPOSE_FILE
# above (COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml:compose.pitr.yaml; the
# file sets BACKUP_PITR=on for the backup service, docs/deployment.md,
# "Point-in-time recovery"). A WAL segment is closed at least this often (seconds):
BACKUP_WAL_ARCHIVE_TIMEOUT=300

# Container logs: per container, at most DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE files of
# DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE, compressed. local is the driver Docker recommends;
# json-file only for a log shipper that reads Docker's log files. The bundled
# one (Alloy, Loki: COMPOSE_PROFILES=logs below) reads through the Docker API
# and works with either.
DOCKER_LOG_DRIVER=local
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE=20m
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE=5

# The web app (links in emails, CORS) and the time zone of cron jobs and emails.
WEB_URL=
CORS_ORIGINS=
APP_TIMEZONE=UTC

# Notifications: the hour (0-23, in each user's own time zone) daily email
# digests are sent; how many days read notifications are kept, and any at all.
NOTIFICATIONS_DIGEST_HOUR=8
NOTIFICATIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=90
NOTIFICATIONS_MAX_AGE_DAYS=365
# Notification mail carries List-Unsubscribe and List-Unsubscribe-Post (RFC 8058,
# one click; needs an https APP_URL). A mailto: address for List-Unsubscribe as
# well, only if your mail setup reads that mailbox (empty: https link only).
MAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_MAILTO=

# Bulk operations (POST /v1/<x>/bulk): up to this many ids run in the
# request; more, and every filter selection, run on the workers.
BULK_SYNC_LIMIT=100

# How many days a user has to cancel the deletion of their account.
ACCOUNT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=14

# API clients' usage per day and route (GET /v1/usage): kept this many days.
USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=400
# Platform analytics (the reporting role's aggregates): a count below this is
# withheld, small-group suppression (docs/tenancy.md, "Platform analytics").
ANALYTICS_MIN_GROUP_SIZE=10

# Deployment switches: platform modules a project may not need, all on. `off`
# leaves the module's routes (they answer 404), workers and jobs out
# (docs/configuration.md, "Deployment switches").
# WEBHOOKS: outgoing webhooks (/v1/webhook-endpoints and deliveries, the delivery worker)
# REPORTS: scheduled reports (/v1/reports, the hourly runner)
# USAGE: API clients' usage metering and daily quotas (quotas then do not apply)
# REALTIME: the socket.io gateway at /realtime
WEBHOOKS=on
REPORTS=on
USAGE=on
REALTIME=on

# Outgoing webhooks: how many days a finished delivery (succeeded, or failed for
# good) is kept, with its attempt log and recorded request, before the nightly
# job deletes it. One still being retried is never deleted.
WEBHOOKS_DELIVERY_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Record revisions (GET /v1/notes/{id}/revisions): kept this many days; each
# record's latest revision always stays.
REVISIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# Exchange rates (docs/money.md): the workers fetch the ECB's euro reference
# rates every working day (off: an app without money). The URL is the
# directory of the ECB's eurofxref documents, or a mirror of it.
EXCHANGE_RATES_FETCH=on
EXCHANGE_RATES_ECB_URL=https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/eurofxref

# Scheduled reports (POST /v1/reports): how long the mailed download link
# works (30m, 24h, 3d; at most 7d). Anyone holding the link (a forwarded
# mail) can download the file until then.
REPORT_LINK_TTL=24h

# Files attached to records (POST /v1/<x>/{id}/attachments): the largest, in
# MB, and the types allowed (detected from the bytes; comma-separated MIME
# types). Empty: images, PDF, Office (docx, xlsx, pptx), text, csv, zip.
ATTACHMENT_MAX_MB=25
ATTACHMENT_ALLOWED_TYPES=

# Users' pictures (PUT /v1/account/avatar): the largest, in MB. PNG, JPEG, WebP
# and GIF, detected from the bytes.
AVATAR_MAX_MB=2

# AI helper (docs/ai.md): off unless the key is set, and off per tenant until
# the `ai.enabled` setting is on. Models are pinned ids. A tenant's daily
# tokens are capped by AI_TENANT_DAILY_TOKENS (0: no cap); identical prompts
# are answered from the cache for AI_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS (0: never).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# AI_BASE_URL=
AI_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
AI_MODEL_CHEAP=claude-haiku-5-5
AI_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1024
AI_ATTEMPTS=2
AI_TENANT_DAILY_TOKENS=500000
AI_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# PDF documents (POST /v1/notes/{id}/pdf; docs/documents.md): the gotenberg
# service the workers render them with. GOTENBERG_URL empty (GOTENBERG_URL=):
# documents are off (503 documents_unavailable); the service still runs
# unless you leave it out. A render that takes longer than
# GOTENBERG_TIMEOUT_MS is tried again, DOCUMENTS_RENDER_ATTEMPTS times in all.
# Gotenberg's limits: GOTENBERG_CPUS, GOTENBERG_MEMORY, GOTENBERG_TMP_SIZE (its
# /tmp, in memory, counted in its memory).
# GOTENBERG_URL=http://gotenberg:3000
GOTENBERG_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
DOCUMENTS_RENDER_ATTEMPTS=3
GOTENBERG_CPUS=1
GOTENBERG_MEMORY=1G
GOTENBERG_TMP_SIZE=512m

# Malware scanning of attachments (docs/attachments.md): off (default, no
# pending state), clamav (clamd; compose.clamav.yaml adds it and sets these for
# api and worker: COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml:compose.clamav.yaml;
# with compose.blue-green.yaml set MALWARE_SCANNER=clamav and CLAMAV_HOST=clamav
# here too, the colours read them from .env) or memory (tests). An upload waits `pending` until scanned; infected files
# are deleted. clamd's StreamMaxLength (CLAMAV_STREAM_MAX_MB, default
# ATTACHMENT_MAX_MB) must be at least the largest attachment. Limits of the
# service: CLAMAV_CPUS, CLAMAV_MEMORY, CLAMAV_TMP_SIZE (512m, in memory: where
# clamd writes each stream it scans; at least the largest attachment, and
# inside CLAMAV_MEMORY).
MALWARE_SCANNER=off
# CLAMAV_HOST=clamav
CLAMAV_PORT=3310
CLAMAV_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
MALWARE_SCAN_ATTEMPTS=5

# Mail: log until you set a provider (smtp + SMTP_URL, or
# resend/postmark/sendgrid + MAIL_API_KEY, or ses).
MAIL_TRANSPORT=log
MAIL_FROM=
SMTP_URL=
MAIL_API_KEY=

# Files (uploads, exports, documents): the local disk (a volume, in the
# backups) or S3 / any S3-compatible store (R2: S3_ENDPOINT, region auto).
# The same AWS_* keys serve SES mail; unset, the AWS packages use the host's
# IAM role. S3_REGION defaults to AWS_REGION.
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_REGION=
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# AWS_REGION=
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Deliverability (docs/deliverability.md): the SNS topic(s) SES publishes
# bounces and complaints to (comma-separated ARNs; empty: the webhook
# refuses everything), subscribed to https://<APP_DOMAIN>/v1/webhooks/mail/ses.
# Addresses that bounce hard or complain stop getting mail; one that bounces
# softly THRESHOLD times (each within WINDOW_HOURS of the last) stops for
# SUPPRESSION_HOURS. Events are kept RETENTION_DAYS. Once every topic has
# SignatureVersion 2, REQUIRE_SIGNATURE_V2=on refuses version 1 (SHA1).
SES_SNS_TOPIC_ARNS=
SES_SNS_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE_V2=off
# Other providers' bounce and complaint webhooks (docs/deliverability.md): empty,
# the route refuses everything. Postmark: the shared secret, as the Basic Auth
# password of https://<APP_DOMAIN>/v1/webhooks/mail/postmark (any user name) or
# in the X-Postmark-Webhook-Secret header. SendGrid: the Event Webhook's
# verification key (base64), webhook URL .../v1/webhooks/mail/sendgrid. Resend:
# the endpoint's signing secret (whsec_...), webhook URL .../v1/webhooks/mail/resend.
POSTMARK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
SENDGRID_WEBHOOK_PUBLIC_KEY=
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_THRESHOLD=3
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_WINDOW_HOURS=24
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_SUPPRESSION_HOURS=72
MAIL_EVENTS_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Push and SMS (docs/notifications.md, "Push and SMS"): both providers are off,
# so nothing leaves until you configure one. Phone numbers and push tokens are
# encrypted at rest: DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS and BLIND_INDEX_KEYS must be set.
# Twilio: inbound STOP/START are posted to https://<APP_DOMAIN>/v1/webhooks/sms/twilio
# (signed with the auth token; TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL when a proxy changes the URL).
# TWILIO_FROM: the sending number (E.164) or a Messaging Service SID (MG...).
SMS_PROVIDER=off
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM=
TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Push providers, comma-separated: off, web-push, apns, fcm, memory (tests).
# `web-push,apns,fcm` serves web, iOS and Android; a device goes to its platform's provider.
# Web Push (VAPID keys: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys); the subject is mailto:ops@example.com or an https URL.
PUSH_PROVIDER=off
WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
WEB_PUSH_SUBJECT=
# APNs (token-based auth): the .p8 key's id and its PEM (newlines written \n), the Apple team id and the
# app's bundle id. APNS_API_URL: https://api.sandbox.push.apple.com for development builds.
APNS_KEY_ID=
APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
APNS_TEAM_ID=
APNS_BUNDLE_ID=
APNS_API_URL=https://api.push.apple.com
# FCM (HTTP v1): the Firebase project id and the service account's client_email and private_key (newlines written \n).
FCM_PROJECT_ID=
FCM_CLIENT_EMAIL=
FCM_PRIVATE_KEY=
FCM_API_URL=https://fcm.googleapis.com
FCM_TOKEN_URL=https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token
# Toll fraud: the country calling-code prefixes SMS may go to (empty: no SMS is
# sent), and the most SMS a UTC day may carry per tenant, per prefix in a tenant,
# and per user (verification codes count).
SMS_ALLOWED_PREFIXES=
SMS_DAILY_LIMIT_PER_TENANT=200
SMS_DAILY_LIMIT_PER_PREFIX=100
SMS_DAILY_LIMIT_PER_USER=10
# ... and per client IP (the route that texts a code also has its own throttle).
SMS_DAILY_LIMIT_PER_IP=20
# Spend: a tenant's cap per UTC month, in minor units of one currency (0: no cap), and the
# price of one SMS segment per prefix (`+1=79,+44=120`; a prefix not listed costs
# SMS_PRICE_DEFAULT_MINOR). Sending stops at the cap; a log error and the
# sms_budget_used_ratio gauge warn at 80 %.
SMS_MONTHLY_BUDGET_MINOR=0
SMS_PRICES=
SMS_PRICE_DEFAULT_MINOR=0
SMS_MAX_LENGTH=320
PUSH_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES=3500
# Devices not seen for this many days are removed every night.
DEVICES_RETENTION_DAYS=270

# The first administrator, created on the first start. ADMIN_PASSWORD empty:
# a password is generated and logged once (`docker compose logs api`). Change
# it after signing in.
#   openssl rand -base64 18
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# on: the first sign-in of an administrator whose address is a placeholder
# (admin@example.com) or whose password was generated is blocked until they
# set a real, verified address and a new password (docs/auth.md,
# "Administrator onboarding"). off: never (scripted installs, tests).
ADMIN_ONBOARDING=on
# The first tenant, made with the first administrator: the operators' tenant
# (the ops API answers only there), and every tenant of a single-tenant
# deployment. More tenants: POST /v1/tenants from it (docs/tenancy.md).
ADMIN_TENANT_NAME=Default
# Forces the deployment's mode (docs/settings.md, "Maintenance and read-only
# mode"): normal, read-only (writes answer 503 read_only, queues pause, cron
# skips) or maintenance (only /health, sign-in and the operators answer).
# Unset: the operators' platform.mode setting decides, at runtime.
# APP_MODE=normal
# multi: operators make more tenants (POST /v1/tenants). single: the first
# tenant is the only one, and a self sign-up (AUTH_SIGN_UP=on) joins it
# (docs/tenancy.md, "Single-tenant deployments").
TENANCY=multi
# TENANCY=single: the roles a sign-up gets (seed role keys, comma-separated,
# e.g. editor; never administrator). Empty: none until an administrator grants.
SIGN_UP_ROLES=
# How long an invitation to a tenant works (hours; default a week).
INVITATION_TTL_HOURS=168
# How many days the operators have to cancel a tenant's deletion (POST
# /v1/tenants/{id}/deletion); deactivated meanwhile, erased after.
TENANT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=30

# Anyone may create an account (POST /v1/auth/sign-up, and a first sign-in
# with a provider); off: accounts come from invitations only.
AUTH_SIGN_UP=off
# TENANCY=multi and AUTH_SIGN_UP=on: a sign-up makes its own tenant, the new
# account its administrator (docs/tenancy.md, "Tenants made at sign-up"). Off:
# only operators make tenants. Ignored with TENANCY=single. Turn bot
# protection on with it (CAPTCHA_MODE).
TENANT_SIGN_UP=off

# Deployment switches (docs/auth.md, "Switches"); a refused route answers 404
# feature_off.
# IMPERSONATION: support impersonation, an administrator acting as a user
# (POST /v1/access/users/{id}/impersonate; IMPERSONATION_TTL_MINUTES long). A
# privileged tool: off unless you opt in. Turning it off ends the running ones.
IMPERSONATION=off
# AUTH_MFA: users may set up two-factor (TOTP, recovery codes). Off, nobody
# can enroll; accounts that already did are still asked for a code (turning
# the switch off never weakens an account).
AUTH_MFA=on
# API_CLIENTS: API clients and their keys (sk_...). Off, their routes are
# refused and no key authenticates; users are unaffected.
API_CLIENTS=on

# Browser sessions: signed out after SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES without a request
# (at least 5) and SESSION_MAX_HOURS after the sign-in, however active. The
# deployment's ceiling: a tenant's auth.sessionIdleMinutes and
# auth.sessionMaxHours (settings) may only shorten these, and the latter also
# shortens token clients' refresh-token families. docs/auth.md, Policies.
SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=120
SESSION_MAX_HOURS=12

# New passwords found in data breaches are refused (Have I Been Pwned's
# Pwned Passwords, k-anonymity: only 5 characters of the password's SHA-1
# are sent). On by default. The API URL can be a self-hosted mirror; when it
# can't be reached in PASSWORD_BREACH_TIMEOUT_MS, the password is allowed
# with a warning (allow) or refused with a 503 (reject). A password seen
# fewer than PASSWORD_BREACH_MIN_COUNT times passes. Sign-in passwords are
# checked too, after the sign-in (never delaying it): a breached one is
# audited and its owner told, once per password and again every
# PASSWORD_BREACH_RECHECK_DAYS. docs/auth.md, Passwords.
PASSWORD_BREACH_CHECK=on
PASSWORD_BREACH_API_URL=https://api.pwnedpasswords.com
PASSWORD_BREACH_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
PASSWORD_BREACH_ON_ERROR=allow
PASSWORD_BREACH_MIN_COUNT=1
PASSWORD_BREACH_RECHECK_DAYS=30

# Password hashing (scrypt: 128 MiB outside the heap and ~0.4 s of CPU each):
# at most this many at once per api process (the worker: 1), the next ones
# wait in order, and past PASSWORD_HASH_MAX_QUEUE waiting or
# PASSWORD_HASH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS of waiting they get 503 server_busy. Raising
# the concurrency needs API_MEMORY raised with it (128 MiB each):
# docs/deployment.md, "Resource limits".
API_PASSWORD_HASH_CONCURRENCY=2
WORKER_PASSWORD_HASH_CONCURRENCY=1
PASSWORD_HASH_MAX_QUEUE=20
PASSWORD_HASH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Bot protection on sign-in (browser and token), sign-up, the magic-link
# request and forgot password (docs/auth.md, "Bot protection"): a Cloudflare
# Turnstile token in X-Captcha-Token, always, or after CAPTCHA_FAILURES
# failed attempts from an address or for an account within a fixed window of
# CAPTCHA_FAILURES_WINDOW_MINUTES from the first one (after-failures). Off by default; the mode on needs both Turnstile keys
# (the dashboard's widget: TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY is public, the secret key can
# be TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY_FILE below). The provider unreachable in
# CAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS: allow the request under CAPTCHA_DEGRADED_LIMIT per
# address in the window (fail-open, with a metric and an alert), or reject
# it with a 503. CAPTCHA_HOSTNAMES: the web app's hosts the widget runs on
# (default: WEB_URL's). Never CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=stub here: production refuses it (only the load test's stack, APP_ENV=loadtest, runs it).
CAPTCHA_MODE=off
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
CAPTCHA_FAILURES=3
CAPTCHA_FAILURES_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
CAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
CAPTCHA_ON_ERROR=allow
CAPTCHA_DEGRADED_LIMIT=2
CAPTCHA_HOSTNAMES=

# Passkeys (WebAuthn; docs/auth.md, "Passkeys"), on by default. The relying
# party id is the web app's domain: empty, WEB_URL's host (else
# APP_DOMAIN). Every credential is bound to it, so choose it before users
# register passkeys and never change it (a parent domain, example.com,
# covers app.example.com too). PASSKEY_ORIGINS: more https origins under it
# where ceremonies run (comma-separated). PASSKEY_RP_NAME: the name
# authenticators show (empty: SERVICE_NAME). Native apps, served at
# /.well-known/assetlinks.json and /.well-known/apple-app-site-association:
# PASSKEY_ANDROID_APPS as package.name:AA:BB:… (the signing certificate's
# SHA-256 fingerprint), PASSKEY_APPLE_APPS as TEAMID.bundle.id, each
# comma-separated. The app refuses to start with an origin outside the RP
# ID, and in production with localhost, an IP address or plain http.
PASSKEYS=on
PASSKEY_RP_ID=
PASSKEY_RP_NAME=
PASSKEY_ORIGINS=
PASSKEY_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECONDS=120
PASSKEY_ANDROID_APPS=
PASSKEY_APPLE_APPS=

# Sign in with a provider (docs/auth.md): each is on when its client id and
# secret are set. Redirect URI to register: https://<APP_DOMAIN>/v1/auth/oidc/<provider>/callback
# (Microsoft Entra ID also needs its tenant's id, a GUID).
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Enterprise SSO (docs/auth.md, "Enterprise SSO"): each tenant sets up its
# own SAML or OpenID Connect connection over the API, nothing here. Its OIDC
# issuer must be a public https:// host; on: also http:// on loopback and
# private networks (development with a local IdP only, never production).
# SSO_ALLOW_LOOPBACK=off

# Idempotency-Key on the routes that create records (@Idempotent()).
#   required  a request without the header is refused (400
#             IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REQUIRED): a retry can never create twice.
#   optional  the header is honoured when sent (a retry replays the first
#             answer; the same key with another body is a 422) but not
#             demanded: simple clients create without keys, and a client that
#             sends none has no duplicate protection. The OpenAPI document
#             follows the mode (the committed typed client is generated with
#             `required`). Routes documented as optional stay so in both.
IDEMPOTENCY=required

# Keys: comma-separated, newest first (add a new one in front to rotate).
# Empty, the app creates IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS, STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS, CURSOR_KEYS and
# JWT_SECRET (and, outside production, MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS) on the first start and keeps them in the
# database (table app_secrets). Set them here to keep them out of the database
# and its backups, or to rotate them.
# WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS is never created: empty, webhook endpoint secrets
# are stored unencrypted. Set it. In production MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS is required
# too (the app refuses to start without it, unless an older deployment still has
# it in app_secrets: move it here).
#   openssl rand -base64 32      (one per name)
IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS=
WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS=
# Seals sorted lists' cursors (nextCursor): a client can't read the sort values.
CURSOR_KEYS=
JWT_SECRET=
# Encrypted columns (docs/encryption.md): the data keys and, separately, the
# blind-index keys. Never created by the app and never kept in the database;
# required at boot once a column is declared encrypted (an SSO connection's
# client secret is: docs/auth.md, "Enterprise SSO"). Rotate by adding a
# key in front; the hourly re-encryption job moves the rows over, and the old
# key leaves once encrypted_rows_on_old_key reads 0.
DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
BLIND_INDEX_KEYS=

# NestJS Observe (traces, metrics, errors), off when empty: the application's
# keys from its project's API keys page.
OBSERVE_APP_KEY=
OBSERVE_APP_SECRET=

# OpenTelemetry (traces and metrics over OTLP/HTTP; Grafana Tempo, Jaeger,
# Honeycomb, Datadog), off when the endpoint is empty. The collector's
# credential goes in OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS (`x-honeycomb-team=KEY`). The
# SDK reads the other standard OTEL_* variables too (OTEL_SERVICE_NAME,
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER). The trace id is in every log line.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=

# Sentry error tracking, off when empty: the project's DSN. Errors only unless
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE (0 to 1) is set. Secrets are scrubbed first.
SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=

# Prometheus metrics at GET /metrics, off (404) when empty: the bearer token
# Prometheus sends (at least 16 characters). Scraped inside the Compose
# network only: Caddy answers /metrics with 404 (docs/deployment.md, Metrics).
#   openssl rand -hex 24
METRICS_TOKEN=

# Not for a deployment: RATE_LIMIT_MULTIPLIER (every rate limit times it,
# default 1) is set by compose.loadtest.yaml for the load test's throwaway
# stack only (docs/development.md, "Load tests"); compose.prod.yaml doesn't
# pass it to the app, and in production the app refuses it above 1 unless
# APP_ENV=loadtest.
# RATE_LIMIT_MULTIPLIER=1

# Alerting (optional): Prometheus, Alertmanager and the PostgreSQL and Redis
# exporters, once compose.monitoring.yaml is added to COMPOSE_FILE above
# (COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml:compose.monitoring.yaml). It
# needs METRICS_TOKEN. Alerts go to any of these; none set, they only show in
# the UIs (docs/deployment.md, Alerting).
# ALERT_EMAIL_TO: comma-separated addresses. The mail goes through
# ALERT_SMTP_URL (empty: SMTP_URL above; smtp:// with STARTTLS or smtps://,
# user and percent-encoded password in it), from ALERT_EMAIL_FROM (empty:
# MAIL_FROM). ALERT_SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=false only for a relay without STARTTLS.
ALERT_EMAIL_TO=
ALERT_SMTP_URL=
ALERT_EMAIL_FROM=
ALERT_SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=true
# A generic webhook (Alertmanager's JSON) and a Slack incoming webhook.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
# The dead man's switch, off when both are empty: the always-firing Watchdog
# alert pings this URL about once a minute (a healthchecks.io check: period
# 1 minute, grace 5 minutes), and that check alarms when the pings stop.
# Preferred: an absolute path to a file outside the repository holding the
# URL, mounted as a Docker secret (readable by the container: chmod 644 in a
# chmod 700 directory). Or the URL itself (the file wins).
ALERT_WATCHDOG_URL_FILE=
ALERT_WATCHDOG_URL=
# The HTTPS probe's host name (TLS server name and Host header for Caddy);
# empty: APP_DOMAIN.
PROBE_HOST=
# The UIs listen on the host's 127.0.0.1 only (reach them through an SSH
# tunnel), and the containers' limits.
PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
ALERTMANAGER_PORT=9093
PROMETHEUS_CPUS=0.5
PROMETHEUS_MEMORY=1G
ALERTMANAGER_CPUS=0.25
ALERTMANAGER_MEMORY=128M

# Log shipping (optional, off): Loki and Alloy read the containers' logs
# (compose.monitoring.yaml), Grafana is the UI. Choose the profiles:
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=logs            Loki + Alloy
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=logs,grafana    and Grafana (needs GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD)
# Works with DOCKER_LOG_DRIVER=local (default) or json-file (docs/deployment.md,
# Logs). Retention in hours (720h = 30 days); Grafana on 127.0.0.1 only.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=
LOKI_RETENTION=720h
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
GRAFANA_PORT=3001
LOKI_CPUS=0.5
LOKI_MEMORY=512M
ALLOY_CPUS=0.25
ALLOY_MEMORY=256M
GRAFANA_CPUS=0.5
GRAFANA_MEMORY=256M

# feature:notes begin
# The example feature: its incoming webhook sender's secret (empty: created on
# the first start, like the keys above; give it to the sender), and how long a
# note waits before it is published.
#   echo "whsec_$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
EXAMPLE_SENDER_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
NOTE_PUBLISH_DELAY=10s
# feature:notes end

# Secrets in files (optional; docs/deployment.md, "Secrets in files"): any
# secret above can live in a file instead, mounted as a Docker secret, so its
# value is in no variable. X_FILE: the file's absolute path, outside the
# repository, readable by the containers (chmod 644 in a chmod 700
# directory); then empty X itself (both set: the service refuses to start).
# A changed file needs `docker compose up -d --force-recreate`. The database
# takes three: POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE (PostgreSQL, backups, the exporter; LF
# line endings, PostgreSQL's image keeps a CR), DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE (the
# owner's URL with that password, postgres://app:<password>@postgres:5432/app,
# mounted on migrate only) and DATABASE_URL_FILE (the app role's URL with
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD's value,
# postgres://app_runtime:<password>@postgres:5432/app; api, worker, migrate).
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD has no file of its own: the whole URL is the secret.
# METRICS_TOKEN stays a variable with compose.monitoring.yaml.
# POSTGRES_MONITOR_PASSWORD_FILE: the monitoring role's password, read by
# migrate (which sets it) and by the PostgreSQL exporter.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=
POSTGRES_MONITOR_PASSWORD_FILE=
# The reporting role's password, read by migrate only (it sets it).
POSTGRES_REPORTING_PASSWORD_FILE=
DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE=
DATABASE_URL_FILE=
# The maintenance role's URL (with POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_PASSWORD's value),
# mounted on migrate and the worker only.
DATABASE_MAINTENANCE_URL_FILE=
REDIS_PASSWORD_FILE=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN_FILE=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
# The age recipient(s) of BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY (public keys), one per line.
BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=
ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=
JWT_SECRET_FILE=
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_FILE=
STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS_FILE=
CURSOR_KEYS_FILE=
IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS_FILE=
WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_FILE=
DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_FILE=
BLIND_INDEX_KEYS_FILE=
SMTP_URL_FILE=
MAIL_API_KEY_FILE=
POSTMARK_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE=
WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
APNS_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
FCM_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY_FILE=
OBSERVE_APP_SECRET_FILE=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_FILE=
# feature:notes begin
EXAMPLE_SENDER_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=
# feature:notes end
